							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=655.msg9736#msg9736">Quote from: Blind Sage on December 29, 2006, 03:37:45 pm</a></div><div class="quote">Not really.&nbsp; <br /><br />People bad mouth him mostly because he&#039;s crude and anti-American, so basically out of their own ignorance.&nbsp; But those who are so fond of him are basically so for the same reason, and then they back it by commenting on his literacy programs and rhetoric about giving oil to America&#039;s poor, while ignoring the more frightening side of him.&nbsp; Do some research beyond leftist periodicals, and you find that he&#039;s no different than most world leaders. He&#039;s a self-interested strongman usuing ideology to shore up his power base.&nbsp; He uses populist rhetoric to excite people, hoping they will ignore how he&#039;s set himself up as a dictator, and they do.<br /><br />Didn&#039;t we already kill this topic?<br /></div><br />Well, if you want to get technical, there is really no such thing as &#039;democracy&#039; anywhere in the world (except for maybe in small Indigeonous areas of Chiapas, Mexico; and they&#039;re pretty limited by the national governement; which is very much a dictatorship; as evidenced by the bloody counter-insurgency by the Mexican army against those people).<br /><br />You have either a dictatorship of the wealthy that is openly brutal (like Russia and Israel), a dictatorship of the wealthy that allows the masses a little room to whine and complain (like most 1st world countries, including England and the United States), then you have dictatorships based upon royal bloodline (like Saudi Arabia and Kuwait), and finally so-called &#039;proletarian dictatorships&#039;, based upon the Marxist-Leninist model like (North Korea, Laos, and Cuba).<br /><br />Based upon a 1st world standard and understanding of &#039;democracy&#039;, Venezula is a &#039;democracy&#039; since Chavez was duly elected, again, with yet another overwhelming majority. This is according to observers in Venezula from the U.S., the U.N., and neighboring South American countries. And for all this talk of his &#039;dictatorship&#039;, it is convienietly forgotten that it was the CIA that funded, armed, and trained the coup attempt by the oil ministers against Chavez.; just as they did with the Bay of Pigs scandal (the CIA-backed attempt to take Cuba from Castro using a poorly trained and equipped militia of right-wing Cuban exiles) by the Kennedy administration.<br /><br />In fact, most of the world&#039;s openly authoritarian regimes are propped up and defended by the United States, including the recently deposed regime of Saddam Hussein; he has been a patron of the U.S. since he took power. He and his conspirators were funded and trained by the CIA, despite the Baath Party&#039;s known ties to the Nazi regime of Adolph Hitler during the 1930&#039;s and 40&#039;s. Only when he decided that he didn&#039;t want to be a pawn for U.S. foreign policy did the United States first sanction him, and then finally decide to overthrow him. Despite all of his evil, he wanted to be free also; specifically, free of foreign domination. This is something anyone can relate to. That&#039;s why the Bush administration cooked up that story of his possession of weapons of mass destruction to get an emotional (re: reactionary), populist response from people in this country and in other first-world, non-Arab/non-Muslim nations.<br /><br />The Kurds asked the United States for help for years before Saddam finally gassed them, but the CIA refused, since the Kurdish resistance was led by the Kurdistan Workers Party, an openly Marxist-Leninist organization. They were put on the U.S.&#039;s terrorism watch list after they carried out attacks on Tthe government of Turkey; another regime that had commited genocide and land theft against them for years. Despite the known history of the Armenian genocide (which, by the way, is illegal to even mention publically in Turkey), Turkey has been a strong ally of the U.S. since the end of World War One.<br /><br />In addition, it is IMPOSSIBLE to be &#039;non-ideological&#039; when holding state power. In the United States, it is the ideology of capitalism, built on a firm base of Freemason doctrine (read James Madison&#039;s &quot;The Federalist Papers&quot; if you have any doubt; this signer of the Declaration of Independence and former U.S. president spells it out the intent and design of the founding fathers very clearly), Christian fundementalism (with occasional flashes of Keyesian liberalism if a Democrat is in the White House or enough poor people of color riot for long enough; refer to the Kerner Commission Report&#039;s recommendations for ending urban uprisings; this book is still in print and deals specifically with the riots in Watts and Newark in the early 1960&#039;s) and a long, unbroken history of white supremacy that guides public policy.<br /><br />European nations and Canada are far less faith-based in their method of rule and offer far more choices at the polls, since they use perportional representation: the more votes your party gets, the more seats you get in parliment.<br />Here Congressional seats are limited by the size of a state&#039;s population and the presidency is decided by the electoral college (an appointed body), NOT the voters (except when the U.S. Surpeme Court APPOINTED Bush president for his first term).<br /><br />It is way past time for real clarity in politics; to &#039;kill&#039; false hope amongst the masses, and to challenge definitions of phenomenon that are created, defined, and defended by the objectively oppressive. A really good question for anyone and everyone to think on is: &quot;do you have ideas...or do ideas have you?&quot;</div>
						</td>
